Actually wait — consider a trailing line with only whitespace: handled by filter(!is_empty after trim). Consider CRLF: lines() strips \r. Consider a lone "\r" at end? lines() strips \r only before \n; a trailing "\r" not followed by \n would remain — then trim removes it. Good.

Let me also double-check: what if the file ends with "49\n" — lines gives no trailing empty. Fine.

I'll write it with a helper for clarity. Keep it compact:

```rust
pub fn total_amount(csv: &str) -> u64 {
    csv.lines()
        .skip(1) // 跳过中文表头
        .map(str::trim)
        .filter(|line| !line.is_empty())
        .map(|line| {
            let field = line.rsplit(',').next().unwrap_or_default().trim();
            field
                .parse::<u64>()
                .unwrap_or_else(|_| panic!("金额列不是整数: {line:?}"))
        })
        .sum()
}
```

Note: `rsplit(',').next()` is always Some for any &str (even empty gives Some("")). But unwrap_or_default on Option<&str>—`Default` for &str is ""? `&str` implements Default returning "". Yes.

Careful: `unwrap_or_else(|_| panic!(...))` — panic! returns `!` which coerces. Fine.

Wait, format string `panic!("金额列不是整数: {line:?}")` — Rust 2021 with format args capture works (edition 2021). Fine.

Also, is skip(1) safe when the first line is a blank line? Then header would be blank and we'd skip the blank, and the actual header row "订单号,客户,金额" would be parsed → panic. Data doesn't have leading blank lines. Requirement: "允许 CRLF 与末尾空行" — only trailing. So skip(1) is correct.

